                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1f5c7b3a-95da-3671-9b16-3819aac181ee",
                        "entry_id": "c5cc5f08-3359-33b5-8a14-5676ef92805c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any chemical compound that is toxic to fish."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4ecdd612-fddf-47dc-9ec3-0dbc6eccf377",
                                "sense_id": "1f5c7b3a-95da-3671-9b16-3819aac181ee",
                                "text": "This dinoflagellate, apparently widely distributed in temperate coastal waters, produces ichthyotoxins that have killed net-pen farmed salmonids in Norway, Scotland, and Ireland (Smayda 2006)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ee972463-8fd3-45c0-8be5-67f7c9edc763",
                                "sense_id": "1f5c7b3a-95da-3671-9b16-3819aac181ee",
                                "text": "While such a pretreatment increased the sensitivity ten to forty times, the damage to the gills induced by brief exposure to ichthyotoxin was reversible."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "def7047c-ad38-4399-92a2-1080db9a5b45",
                                "sense_id": "1f5c7b3a-95da-3671-9b16-3819aac181ee",
                                "text": "The rate of the ichthyotoxic effect on immersed minnows within the temperature range of 10–30°C increases directly with the temperature (from 3 hours to 30 minutes), but the final titer of the ichthyotoxin is identical at both high and low temperatures."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "51792153-abf1-3151-94d3-2b62711b41a8",
                        "entry_id": "c5cc5f08-3359-33b5-8a14-5676ef92805c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any toxin produced by fish."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9bcdcab8-7225-4faf-b196-a66db8d6ed7d",
                                "sense_id": "51792153-abf1-3151-94d3-2b62711b41a8",
                                "text": "More recently, Ehrhardt (1971) drew attention to ichthyotoxins as a whole. Among fish alone there are more than 500 species that are dangerous to man."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "662a5de7-f860-4c73-8078-f23a9fa1cd7e",
                                "sense_id": "51792153-abf1-3151-94d3-2b62711b41a8",
                                "text": "Ichthyotoxins are sufficiently widely present in the reef fish of tropical oceans, and with a sufficiently unpredictable occurrence, as to render the large-scale commercialization of snappers, groupers, jack, and other large, marketable fish a chancy business; in some iland groups, such as (it is claimed) Johnston and Phenix, very high percentages of species are toxic."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "388d1eb3-0b20-4657-ae5a-b1a8a4907b6e",
                                "sense_id": "51792153-abf1-3151-94d3-2b62711b41a8",
                                "text": "Ciguatoxin (CTX) is the principal ichthyotoxin involved in a complex human food poisoning known as ciguatera, characterized mainly by neurological, gastrointestinal, and, in the most severe cases, cardiovascular disorders (6—10)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
